---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Framing Hammers in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We drove 2,000 sixteen penny nails with seven framing hammers. Titanium versus steel, milled versus smooth face, and what handles do to your elbow.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "stiletto-tb15mc",
    name: "Stiletto TB15MC TiBone",
    category: "Titanium, 15 oz, Milled Face, Replaceable Face",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 1400,
    pros: [
      "Titanium head transmits dramatically less shock to the arm than steel",
      "Drives nails as well as a much heavier steel hammer",
      "Replaceable milled face, so the striking surface can be renewed",
      "Very well balanced with a comfortable, controlled swing",
      "Noticeably less fatiguing across a full day of framing",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Extraordinarily expensive for a hammer",
      "Titanium is softer, so the face wears faster than steel",
      "Overkill for anyone not framing regularly",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The hammer professional framers save up for. Titanium changes how your arm feels at the end of the day.",
    description:
      "Titanium framing hammers cost several times what steel ones do, which invites scepticism until you understand what is being bought. When a steel head strikes a nail, a substantial proportion of the energy reflects back up the handle into your hand, wrist and elbow. Titanium returns far less of it — the commonly quoted figure is a small fraction of what steel transmits — while delivering comparable energy into the nail. After a thousand nails that difference is not subtle. In our testing the Stiletto TiBone at 15 ounces drove sixteen penny nails in a similar number of strokes to steel hammers weighing over twice as much, and the testers who used it reported markedly less forearm and elbow fatigue at the end of the day. For anyone framing regularly, particularly anyone who has already developed elbow problems, that is the whole argument. The milled face is replaceable, which matters because titanium is softer than steel and the waffle pattern wears faster. Being able to renew the striking surface rather than the hammer extends its life considerably. The objections are price and durability. It costs more than most power tools, and the softer material means the face is a consumable. For occasional use, a good steel hammer does the job.",
  },
  {
    id: "estwing-e3-22s",
    name: "Estwing E3-22S",
    category: "Steel, 22 oz, Milled Face, One Piece Forged",
    badge: "Best Steel",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 14600,
    pros: [
      "One piece forged construction that will essentially never break",
      "Excellent 22 ounce driving power for sixteen penny nails",
      "Shock reduction grip that helps considerably for a steel hammer",
      "Legendary durability with decades of field reputation",
      "Very reasonable price for the quality",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Steel transmits far more shock than titanium",
      "Heavier swing is tiring over a full day",
      "One piece construction means a damaged handle cannot be replaced",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The default professional steel hammer. Nearly indestructible, drives well, and priced sensibly.",
    description:
      "Wooden and fibreglass handled hammers can separate from their heads, which is both dangerous and inconvenient on a job site. Estwing solved that permanently by forging the head and handle from a single piece of steel, and the result has been the default framing hammer for generations of carpenters. There is essentially no failure mode: the tool does not come apart, the handle does not crack, and the head does not loosen. At 22 ounces it has the mass to set a sixteen penny nail in a small number of strokes, and the milled face grips the nail head so glancing blows are rare. The shock reduction grip is genuinely effective by steel hammer standards, absorbing a useful proportion of the vibration that one piece steel construction would otherwise transmit directly to the hand. Price is very reasonable for the quality. The unavoidable limitation is the material. Steel returns far more energy to your arm than titanium, and the fact that the handle is also steel means there is no wooden or fibreglass shaft to damp it. Across a full day of framing, the difference in fatigue against a titanium hammer is real. And because it is one piece, a damaged handle means a new hammer rather than a new handle.",
  },
  {
    id: "martinez-m1",
    name: "Martinez M1 Titanium",
    category: "Titanium Handle, Steel Head, 15 oz Head, Replaceable",
    badge: "Best Hybrid",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 900,
    pros: [
      "Titanium handle with a replaceable steel head, combining the best of both",
      "Steel face lasts far longer than a titanium striking surface",
      "Excellent shock reduction through the titanium shaft",
      "Heads available in several weights and face patterns",
      "Superb build quality with a strong following among professionals",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very expensive",
      "Replacement heads add ongoing cost",
      "Somewhat scarce, often on backorder",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Titanium where it reduces shock and steel where it takes the beating. The most sensible expensive hammer here.",
    description:
      "The weakness of an all-titanium hammer is that titanium is comparatively soft, so the striking face wears faster than steel and eventually needs replacing. The weakness of an all-steel hammer is that steel transmits shock. Martinez separates the two functions: a titanium handle that absorbs vibration on its way to your arm, and a replaceable steel head that takes the impact and lasts. It is the most logical arrangement in the category and it is why these hammers have a devoted following among professional framers. In use it delivers most of the fatigue reduction of an all-titanium hammer, because the shaft is where most of the damping happens, while the steel face survives the sort of abuse — striking nail pullers, hitting the odd screw, prying — that would mark titanium. Heads are available in several weights and in both milled and smooth faces, so the same handle adapts to different work. Build quality is excellent throughout. The problems are cost and availability. It is very expensive, replacement heads represent an ongoing cost, and these hammers are frequently on backorder because production is limited. For a professional it is defensible; for a homeowner it is an extravagance.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-dwht51054",
    name: "DEWALT DWHT51054",
    category: "Steel, 22 oz, Milled Face, Rubber Grip",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 7300,
    pros: [
      "Good driving power at a competitive price",
      "Effective rubber overmould grip that reduces shock reasonably",
      "Milled face grips nail heads well",
      "Magnetic nail starter allows one handed starting",
      "Widely available",
    ],
    cons: [
      "More shock transmitted than premium hammers",
      "Balance is slightly head heavy for some users",
      "Grip wears over time and cannot be replaced",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A capable 22 ounce steel hammer with a magnetic nail start, at a price that makes sense for occasional framing.",
    description:
      "For someone framing a shed, a deck or a partition wall rather than houses for a living, a premium hammer is difficult to justify. The DEWALT DWHT51054 covers that requirement with a 22 ounce steel head, a milled face and a rubber overmould grip, at a price that requires no deliberation. In our testing it set sixteen penny nails in a comparable number of strokes to the Estwing, which is what a 22 ounce head should do. The magnetic nail starter is more useful than it sounds. A slot in the top of the head holds a nail so it can be set with a single one handed swing, which matters when you are up a ladder or reaching overhead with one hand holding a board. Framers who use it regularly consider it close to essential. The rubber grip absorbs a reasonable amount of shock for a steel hammer. Where it sits behind the premium options is fatigue and refinement. Steel transmits more shock than titanium, and the grip, while effective, is not the equal of a fibreglass or titanium shaft. The balance is slightly head heavy, which some users like and some do not. The grip wears with use and cannot be replaced.",
  },
  {
    id: "vaughan-999",
    name: "Vaughan 999 California Framer",
    category: "Steel, 23 oz, Hickory Handle, Milled Face",
    badge: "Best Wood Handle",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 3800,
    pros: [
      "Hickory handle absorbs shock better than steel and can be replaced when damaged",
      "Classic balance that many carpenters strongly prefer",
      "Long handle for extra leverage and reach",
      "Milled face grips nails securely",
      "Reasonable price for a professional grade hammer",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Wooden handles can break, though they are replaceable",
      "Handle needs occasional attention to stay tight",
      "Less weather resistant than steel or fibreglass",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The traditional framer, and hickory is genuinely better at absorbing shock than steel. Handles are replaceable.",
    description:
      "Before fibreglass and titanium, framing hammers had hickory handles, and a significant number of carpenters still insist they are the best. There is a real basis for it: hickory has natural damping properties that absorb a meaningful proportion of impact energy before it reaches your hand, considerably more than a one piece steel hammer does. It is also replaceable, so a broken handle costs a few dollars rather than a new hammer. The Vaughan 999 is the classic California framer, with a long handle that gives extra leverage and reach for toe nailing and working at full reach. The 23 ounce head sets sixteen penny nails efficiently and the milled face grips reliably. Balance is what many carpenters describe as correct, which is subjective and consistently reported. The price is reasonable for a professional grade tool. The reservations are all about the handle. Wood can break, particularly on an over-strike where the shaft rather than the face contacts something. It can loosen and needs occasional attention with a wedge. And it is less tolerant of being left out in the rain than steel or fibreglass. For a carpenter who looks after tools, none of that is significant.",
  },
  {
    id: "stanley-fatmax-antivibe",
    name: "Stanley FatMax AntiVibe 22 oz",
    category: "Steel, 22 oz, Torsion Control Grip, Rip Claw",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 9600,
    pros: [
      "Low price for a capable framing hammer",
      "AntiVibe design reduces vibration measurably for the price",
      "One piece forged construction with no head to loosen",
      "Comfortable grip that does not slip when wet",
      "Widely stocked",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Still transmits more shock than better hammers",
      "Face pattern wears faster than premium tools",
      "Balance is less refined than professional hammers",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Solid framing capability for very little money. The vibration control is real, if not premium.",
    description:
      "Most people who need a framing hammer need it for a specific project rather than a career, and for that a hammer that drives nails reliably and does not hurt too much is the whole requirement. The Stanley FatMax AntiVibe delivers on both at a price well below the professional tools. The one piece forged construction means there is no head to work loose, and the AntiVibe design — a tuned tip in the handle intended to cancel vibration — does produce a measurably less harsh feel than a plain steel hammer. The grip is comfortable and stays secure when hands are wet or sweaty, which matters more on a roof than in a workshop. At 22 ounces it has the mass to set sixteen penny nails without excessive strokes. It is stocked almost everywhere. Where it falls short is refinement and durability. Shock transmission is better than a bare steel hammer and clearly worse than a titanium or hickory handled one, so a full day of framing still tells. The milled face pattern wears faster than premium tools, gradually losing its grip on nail heads. Balance is functional rather than pleasant. For a project hammer, it is very good value.",
  },
  {
    id: "estwing-emrf20s",
    name: "Estwing EMRF20S Ultra Framer",
    category: "Steel Head, Fibreglass Handle, 20 oz, Milled Face",
    badge: "Best Lightweight",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 4200,
    pros: [
      "Lighter 20 ounce head that is less tiring over a long day",
      "Fibreglass handle absorbs more shock than one piece steel",
      "Long handle giving leverage that offsets the lighter head",
      "Weather resistant and dimensionally stable",
      "Well balanced with a comfortable swing",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Lighter head needs an extra stroke on larger nails",
      "Fibreglass can be damaged by repeated over-strikes",
      "Handle is not replaceable",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The compromise that suits most people. Lighter than a 22 ounce, with a shaft that damps better than steel.",
    description:
      "The instinct when buying a framing hammer is to go heavy, on the theory that more mass drives nails faster. It does, up to a point, and past that point the extra weight costs more in fatigue than it saves in strokes. Many experienced framers have moved down in weight over their careers for exactly this reason. The Estwing Ultra Framer at 20 ounces, with a long fibreglass handle, is that compromise done well: the extra handle length provides leverage that recovers much of the driving power a lighter head gives up. Fibreglass damps better than one piece steel, absorbing a useful proportion of the impact before it reaches the hand, and it is dimensionally stable and weather resistant in a way wood is not. Balance is good and the swing feels controlled rather than effortful. The compromises are modest. A 20 ounce head occasionally needs an extra stroke on a large nail in dense timber. Fibreglass tolerates over-strikes less well than hickory and can be damaged over time by repeatedly hitting the shaft against the work, and unlike a wooden handle it cannot be replaced. For most people, this is the sensible weight and format.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Framing Hammer",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-framing-hammer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best framing hammer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Stiletto TB15MC TiBone if you frame for a living, because titanium transmits far less shock to your arm across thousands of nails. Estwing E3-22S is the best steel hammer and nearly indestructible, and Estwing EMRF20S is the sensible compromise for most people.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a titanium hammer worth the money?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a professional framer or anyone with existing elbow problems, yes. Titanium returns a small fraction of the shock steel does, which over thousands of nails is a real difference in fatigue and joint strain. For occasional framing, a good steel hammer is far better value.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What weight framing hammer should I buy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Twenty to twenty-two ounces suits most people, balancing driving power against a swing you can sustain all day. Heavier heads drive faster and tire you sooner. A longer handle recovers much of the power a lighter head gives up, which is why 20 ounce long handled hammers are popular.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Milled face or smooth face?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Milled for framing. The waffle pattern grips the nail head so off-centre blows still drive rather than glancing off, which matters enormously over thousands of nails. It also stamps the pattern into any timber it touches, so use a smooth face for finish work and trim.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Rip claw or curved claw?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Rip claw for framing. The straight claw is used for prying boards apart, splitting timber and demolition, which is most of what a framer does with the back of the hammer. Curved claws pull nails more easily and are less useful for everything else.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I avoid hammer elbow?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Hold the handle near the end with a relaxed grip, firming up only at impact, and swing from the elbow and shoulder rather than flicking the wrist. A hammer with a damping handle, hickory, fibreglass or titanium, transmits far less shock than one piece steel.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools",
    tags: [
      "framing hammer",
      "titanium hammer",
      "hand tools",
      "carpentry",
      "milled face",
      "construction",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Framing Hammers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We drove roughly 2,000 sixteen penny nails into Douglas fir with seven hammers, counting strokes per nail and tracking how each one felt by the end of the day.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#stiletto-tb15mc"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#titanium"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Titanium vs Steel
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#weight" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Head Weight</a>
          <a href="#titanium" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Titanium vs Steel</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#face" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Milled vs Smooth Face</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Hammer Technique</a>
          <a href="#nailgun" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Hammer vs Nail Gun</a>
          <a href="#features" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Features That Matter</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A framing hammer is a personal tool in a way most tools are not, because the wrong one produces elbow pain that lasts long after the job. The three variables that matter — head weight, head material and handle material — all trade driving power against how much shock reaches your arm.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Head Weight and Why Heavier Is Not Better -->
        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Head Weight and Why Heavier Is Not Better
          </h2>

          <p>
            Head weight is the specification everyone looks at first, and the relationship between weight and productivity is not linear.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">More Mass Means Fewer Strokes</h3>

          <p>
            A heavier head delivers more energy per blow, so a sixteen penny nail that takes four strokes with a 16 ounce hammer might take two or three with a 22 ounce one. On a framing job with thousands of nails that is real time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">More Mass Also Means More Fatigue</h3>

          <p>
            Every one of those swings has to be lifted and controlled, and the shock returned per blow is larger too. Past a certain weight the fatigue cost exceeds the stroke saving, and where that point falls depends on the person.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Twenty to Twenty-Two Ounces Suits Most People</h3>

          <p>
            This is the range most professional framing hammers occupy, and it balances driving power against a swing that can be sustained. Very heavy hammers in the 28 ounce range exist and are specialist tools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Handle Length Substitutes for Weight</h3>

          <p>
            A longer handle increases head speed for the same effort, which is why a lighter head on a long handle can drive nails as effectively as a heavier one on a short handle, while being less tiring to control.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Titanium Changes the Calculation Entirely</h3>

          <p>
            Because titanium returns so much less energy to the arm, a 15 ounce titanium head can be swung harder and more often than a 22 ounce steel one, which is how a much lighter hammer drives comparably.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Framing Hammer Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-22 oz</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Weight range that suits most framers</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">15 oz</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical titanium head weight</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">16d</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Nail size a framing hammer is sized for</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">2-3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Strokes per nail with a good hammer</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
