        <!-- SECTION 2: Titanium vs Steel -->
        <div id="titanium" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Titanium vs Steel
          </h2>

          <p>
            The material of the head and handle determines how much of each blow ends up in the nail and how much ends up in your elbow.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Steel returns a lot of energy to your arm.</strong> When a steel head hits a nail, a substantial fraction of the impact reflects back up the handle. That is shock, and cumulatively it is what causes the elbow and wrist problems common among carpenters.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Titanium returns far less.</strong> The commonly cited figure is a small fraction of steel's shock transmission, with more of the energy going into the nail. In practice that means less fatigue for the same work, which is the entire reason these hammers cost what they do.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Titanium is softer, so the face wears.</strong> The milled waffle pattern on a titanium face degrades faster than on steel. Better titanium hammers use a replaceable face or a steel head on a titanium handle for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hickory and fibreglass sit in between.</strong> Both damp considerably better than one piece steel while costing a fraction of titanium. For most people, a steel head on a wooden or fibreglass handle is the sensible middle ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The handle matters as much as the head.</strong> A one piece steel hammer has no damping element at all between the face and your hand, which is why they feel harsher than a steel head on a wooden shaft.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy titanium if you already have elbow trouble.</strong> The economic case is weak for occasional use and strong for anyone whose living depends on their arm working. That is the honest division.
          </p>
        </div>

