        <!-- SECTION 4: Milled Face or Smooth Face -->
        <div id="face" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Milled Face or Smooth Face
          </h2>

          <p>
            The face pattern is a simple choice with consequences that are obvious the first time you get it wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Milled faces grip the nail head.</strong> A waffle pattern of small pyramids bites into the nail head, so a slightly off-centre blow still drives rather than glancing off. For framing, where speed matters more than appearance, this is the correct choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Milled faces mark the wood.</strong> Any blow that lands on the timber leaves the waffle pattern stamped into it. On framing lumber that will be covered by drywall this is irrelevant; on anything visible it is permanent damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Smooth faces leave a clean surface.</strong> For finish carpentry, trim, and any work that will be seen, a smooth face is essential. It also grips the nail less, so glancing blows and bent nails are more common.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A framing hammer should be milled.</strong> If you are choosing one hammer for framing, choose milled. The grip advantage is significant when driving thousands of nails, and framing lumber does not need to look good.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Own both if you do both kinds of work.</strong> A milled framing hammer and a smooth faced 16 ounce finish hammer covers everything, and using the right one is faster than compensating with the wrong one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Milled patterns wear out.</strong> The pyramids flatten with use, gradually losing grip. On premium hammers with replaceable faces this is a consumable; on others it eventually means a new hammer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Swinging Efficiently -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Swinging Efficiently
          </h2>

          <p>
            Framing is repetitive work, and technique determines both how fast you go and whether your arm still works in ten years.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Grip near the end of the handle.</strong> Choking up shortens the effective lever and costs head speed, which means more strokes for the same result. Hold near the end and let the hammer do the work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Swing from the elbow and shoulder, not the wrist.</strong> Driving with wrist flexion concentrates strain in the smallest joint involved. A relaxed swing pivoting from the elbow uses larger muscles and produces more head speed with less effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hold the handle loosely until impact.</strong> A tight grip throughout transmits far more shock into the hand and forearm. Let the hammer travel with a relaxed grip and firm up only at the moment of contact.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Set the nail with a light tap, then drive.</strong> One controlled tap to seat the nail, then full strokes. Trying to drive from the first blow is how nails bend and thumbs get hit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use the magnetic starter for overhead and one handed work.</strong> Setting a nail single handed at arm's length or above your head is far safer with a magnetic slot than with fingers holding the nail.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stop when your elbow complains.</strong> Tendon damage from repetitive impact develops gradually and heals slowly. Pain during or after a day of framing is a signal to change hammer, technique or pace rather than to push through.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Loose Grip, Long Handle</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Two habits do more for framing endurance than any hammer. Hold the handle near its end, which gives you the full lever and more head speed for the same effort. And keep the grip relaxed through the swing, firming up only at impact — a white knuckle grip pipes the shock of every blow straight into your forearm and elbow.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Hammer or Nail Gun -->
        <div id="nailgun" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Hammer or Nail Gun
          </h2>

          <p>
            Framing nailers exist and are faster, which raises the reasonable question of whether a framing hammer is still a necessary purchase. It is, and the reasons are worth spelling out.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A nailer is faster on repetitive runs.</strong> Sheathing a wall, decking a floor, or nailing off a roof are jobs where a pneumatic or cordless nailer is several times quicker and far less tiring. Nobody frames a house by hand for speed.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A hammer does everything a nailer cannot.</strong> Persuading a stud into place, prying apart a mis-nailed joint, driving a nail into an awkward corner the gun will not reach, and demolition are all hammer work, and they come up constantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Setup time favours the hammer on small jobs.</strong> A compressor, a hose and a nailer take longer to set up than a deck repair takes to do. For anything under a few dozen nails, the hammer is genuinely faster start to finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Toe nailing by hand is often more controllable.</strong> Driving a nail at an angle into the edge of a stud is a job guns do inconsistently and a competent framer does reliably by hand, which is why hammers stay on the belt.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Nailers depend on power and consumables.</strong> No compressor, no battery, wrong nail collation, jammed magazine — a hammer has none of these failure modes and always works.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Most framers carry both.</strong> The gun does the volume and the hammer does everything else, and neither replaces the other. Buying a nailer is not a reason to buy a cheap hammer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Claws, Starters and Other Features -->
        <div id="features" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Claws, Starters and Other Features
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beyond weight and material, a handful of details separate hammers that suit framing from those that do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Rip claws, not curved claws.</strong> A straight rip claw is used for prying boards apart, splitting timber and demolition, which is most of what a framer does with the back of a hammer. Curved claws are better at pulling nails and worse at everything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Magnetic nail starters earn their place.</strong> A slot that holds a nail for a one handed start is genuinely useful up a ladder, overhead, or anywhere the other hand is holding something. Framers who use them rarely go back.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Side nail pullers help with bent nails.</strong> A notch in the side of the head grips a nail that has bent over, which the claw cannot get under. Small feature, frequently used.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Grip texture matters when wet.</strong> Hands get sweaty and rain happens. A grip that becomes slippery is both slower and dangerous, and it is worth checking how a hammer feels with a wet hand before committing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Overstrike protection extends handle life.</strong> The area just below the head takes the hits that miss, and a reinforced collar there is what prevents a handle failing early. Wooden handles suffer most from this.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Head geometry affects reach into a bay.</strong> A slimmer head and a longer neck let you drive a nail in a tight stud bay or against a plate where a fat head fouls the adjacent timber. It is a detail nobody mentions until the hammer will not fit where the nail needs to go.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A hatchet claw suits some framers.</strong> Some framing hammers carry a straighter, sharper claw usable for rough splitting and prying siding. It is more aggressive than a standard rip claw and it wears the timber it touches, so it is a preference rather than an upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Handle length is a real variable.</strong> Longer handles give more leverage and reach at the cost of control in tight spaces. Sixteen to eighteen inches is typical for framing, and the difference between the ends of that range is noticeable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best framing hammer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stiletto TB15MC TiBone if you frame for a living, because titanium transmits far less shock to your arm across thousands of nails. Estwing E3-22S is the best steel hammer and nearly indestructible, and Estwing EMRF20S is the sensible compromise for most people.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a titanium hammer worth the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a professional framer or anyone with existing elbow problems, yes. Titanium returns a small fraction of the shock steel does, which over thousands of nails is a real difference in fatigue and joint strain. For occasional framing, a good steel hammer is far better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight framing hammer should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Twenty to twenty-two ounces suits most people, balancing driving power against a swing you can sustain all day. Heavier heads drive faster and tire you sooner. A longer handle recovers much of the power a lighter head gives up, which is why 20 ounce long handled hammers are popular.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Milled face or smooth face?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Milled for framing. The waffle pattern grips the nail head so off-centre blows still drive rather than glancing off, which matters enormously over thousands of nails. It also stamps the pattern into any timber it touches, so use a smooth face for finish work and trim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Rip claw or curved claw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rip claw for framing. The straight claw is used for prying boards apart, splitting timber and demolition, which is most of what a framer does with the back of the hammer. Curved claws pull nails more easily and are less useful for everything else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I avoid hammer elbow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hold the handle near the end with a relaxed grip, firming up only at impact, and swing from the elbow and shoulder rather than flicking the wrist. A hammer with a damping handle, hickory, fibreglass or titanium, transmits far less shock than one piece steel.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Framing Hammer Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hammer mistakes cost you speed in the short term and joint health in the long term.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Heaviest Hammer Available</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                More mass drives nails in fewer strokes and tires you faster, and past about 22 ounces the fatigue usually costs more than the strokes saved. Many experienced framers move down in weight over their careers. A longer handle recovers most of the power a lighter head gives up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Gripping Tightly Through the Swing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A white knuckle grip pipes the shock of every blow directly into the hand, forearm and elbow. Letting the hammer travel with a relaxed grip and firming up only at the moment of impact reduces transmitted shock substantially, and it costs nothing to change.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choking Up on the Handle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Holding near the head feels more controlled and shortens the lever, which reduces head speed and means more strokes for every nail. Gripping near the end of the handle gives the full leverage the tool was designed around and does more work for the same effort.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Milled Face on Finish Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The waffle pattern that grips nail heads so effectively also stamps itself permanently into any timber it touches. On framing lumber destined to be covered that is fine; on trim, cabinetry or anything visible it is damage that cannot be sanded out.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

